<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amulius</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amulius is a legendary king of Alba Longa in Roman tradition, who seized the throne from his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a>. To prevent rivals, he made Numitor's daughter Rhea Silvia a Vestal Virgin, and when she bore the twins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus he ordered them to be drowned. According to the legend the twins survived, grew up, killed Amulius and restored Numitor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amulius.jpg" alt="Amulius" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Constantine Manasses · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:21-manasses-chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In Roman mythology Amulius was king of Alba Longa, son of Procas and brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a>. He ordered the death of his infant twin grandnephews <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and Remus, and was deposed and killed by them when they grew up. Tradition gave him a reign of 41 years. According to the historian Dionysius of Halicarnassus, who wrote in the first century BCE, Procas willed the throne to Numitor, but Amulius deposed him. Fearing a threat to his rule, he had Numitor's son Aegestus killed and blamed thieves, while Numitor, who knew the truth, feigned ignorance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Amulius appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a>'s daughter Rhea Silvia a Vestal priestess, so that her vow of chastity would prevent her having sons who might overthrow him. She nevertheless became pregnant, and in most tellings the father was the god Mars. In one source Amulius himself, in full armour, committed the rape. He had her watched and placed under guard, and she was either put to death or kept in a hidden dungeon.</p><p>Dionysius, citing Fabius Pictor, Cincius, Cato and Piso, writes that the king ordered the twins thrown into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>. Floodwater kept his servants from the stream, so they left the basket in standing water at the site of the ficus Ruminalis. A she-wolf found and nursed the twins at her lair, the Lupercal, and Amulius' servant Faustulus took them home to raise.</p><p>According to Fabius, when the twins were 18 they quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a>'s herdsmen, and Remus was captured in an ambush. Faustulus told <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> the truth of their birth, and Numitor, to whom Amulius had handed Remus for punishment, told Remus the same. When Faustulus was caught by Alban guards carrying the basket into the city, he gave a false account to Amulius.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The twins and their grandfather led their supporters to the palace, killed Amulius and took control of the city. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numitor">Numitor</a> was restored as king of Alba Longa, and the twins went on to found <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Amulius' story is told in several versions, and the ancient accounts differ on details such as the twins' discovery, Rhea Silvia's fate and who saved the basket.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amulius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Romans described a line of Alban kings, from Silvius to Numitor and his usurping brother <b>Amulius</b>, but these are legendary.</blockquote><a href="/state/alba-longa">Alba Longa</a></li></ul></section>
